\paragraph{Grounding ablation: death is a structural vulnerability.}
Direct intervention on the dominant death action confirms that the vulnerability is not trivially fixable. An explicit prompt warning (``use \texttt{focus on} only for final answers'') reduces death rate from 48.0\% to 37.0\% ($\Delta = -11$~pp), and hard action-space filtering that blocks \texttt{focus on} during exploration (379 blocks across 200 episodes, zero leaks) reduces it further to 29.0\% ($\Delta = -19$~pp). The gradient $48.0 \to 37.0 \to 29.0 \to 16.3\%$ (deterministic) shows that each layer captures a portion of the vulnerability but none eliminates it: even with perfect exploration-phase filtering, agents submit \texttt{focus on} to incorrect objects during the answer window, leaving a 12.7~pp gap to deterministic execution comparable to the residual gaps from Reflexion (34.0\%) and 72B scaling (34.5\%). Per-task patterns vary: \texttt{identify-life-stages} and \texttt{inclined-plane} approach deterministic-level safety under filtering (${\sim}$15--20\% DR), while \texttt{chemistry-mix} and \texttt{find-animal} remain at 35--40\%, indicating that grounding difficulty is task-dependent.

